{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ZYV1oXyaLHjVk39snGekp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\"uuid\": \"ada14c03-e046-5716-adaf-06898bc15ce3\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.087, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n2\t{\"uuid\": \"8c5c94b5-9fee-535f-843e-b04ee1988521\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 32, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.1, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n3\t{\"uuid\": \"b61ee9a7-7ecf-5d80-b62a-1e41323c458e\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 16, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.094, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n4\t{\"uuid\": \"2c1d8396-ecc3-56cb-b46a-173371d7b576\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 1024}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.09300000000000001, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n5\t{\"uuid\": \"8b677344-0499-5ef8-93d8-b0b048bfaa4b\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 4, \"seq_len\": 512}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.09300000000000001, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n6\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f843108-68c0-43be-addb-b7c1183c67b7","uuid":"3e385f77-9a08-4ab3-abcf-a9e73ad83be2","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:31:32.601Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-008_expert_output_weighted_index_add_accumulation/task/feedback_workloads.jsonl","content":"{\"uuid\": \"ada14c03-e046-5716-adaf-06898bc15ce3\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.087, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"8c5c94b5-9fee-535f-843e-b04ee1988521\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 32, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.1, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"b61ee9a7-7ecf-5d80-b62a-1e41323c458e\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 16, \"seq_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.094, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"2c1d8396-ecc3-56cb-b46a-173371d7b576\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 1024}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.09300000000000001, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"8b677344-0499-5ef8-93d8-b0b048bfaa4b\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 4, \"seq_len\": 512}, \"inputs\": {\"final_hidden_states\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"expert_outputs\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"token_indices\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.09300000000000001, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ScmiwbpmnmScv2BqwCYuMh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/008 Expert Output Weighted Index-Add Accumulation\n2\t\n3\tTask ID: `sol_execbench :: L1/008_expert_output_weighted_index_add_accumulation`\n4\tTarget GPU: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere), HBM2e ~2.0 TB/s, L2 = 40 MB.\n5\tPrimary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata / launch plumbing.\n6\tNo Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. What the operation actually computes\n11\t\n12\tReference (`task/definition.json`):\n13\t\n14\t```python\n15\t@torch.no_grad()\n16\tdef run(final_hidden_states, expert_outputs, token_indices):\n17\t    output = final_hidden_states.clone()\n18\t    output.index_add_(dim=0, index=token_indices, source=expert_outputs)\n19\t    return output\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tSemantically, for `i in [0, num_selected_tokens)`:\n23\t\n24\t```\n25\toutput[token_indices[i], :] += expert_outputs[i, :]\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\twith the base value `final_hidden_states` copied first, and **atomic add** semantics\n29\tbecause the destination indices contain duplicates (top-k routing → many source rows\n30\tmap to the same token row).\n31\t\n32\t### Tensors / dtypes / shapes\n33\t\n34\t| name                 | shape                          | dtype    | role                          |\n35\t|----------------------|--------------------------------|----------|-------------------------------|\n36\t| `final_hidden_states`| `[M, H]`                       | bf16     | base accumulation buffer      |\n37\t| `expert_outputs`     | `[N, H]`                       | bf16     | per-selected-token weighted output (already weighted) |\n38\t| `token_indices`      | `[N]`                          | int64    | destination row for each source row |\n39\t| `output` (return)    | `[M, H]`                       | bf16     | `clone(base)` + scatter-add   |\n40\t\n41\tConstants: `H = hidden_size = 3072`, `num_experts_per_tok = 8`.\n42\tRelations: `M = batch_seq_len = batch_size * seq_len`, `N = num_selected_tokens = 8 * M`.\n43\t\n44\tImportant: the routing weight multiply has **already been folded into** `expert_outputs`\n45\t(the description says \"weighted outputs\"; `run` does a plain `index_add_`). So this kernel\n46\tis a pure **scatter-add**, not a fused multiply-scatter. No extra weight tensor is provided.\n47\t\n48\t### Feedback workload sizes\n49\t\n50\t| # | uuid | batch | seq | M (rows) | N=8M | src read (bf16) | out (bf16) | fp32 scratch | atol |\n51\t|---|------|-------|-----|----------|------|-----------------|------------|--------------|------|\n52\t| 1 | ada14c03 | 2  | 256  | 512  | 4096  | 25.2 MB  | 3.1 MB  | 6.3 MB   | 0.087 |\n53\t| 2 | 8c5c94b5 | 32 | 256  | 8192 | 65536 | 402.7 MB | 50.3 MB | 100.7 MB | 0.100 |\n54\t| 3 | b61ee9a7 | 16 | 256  | 4096 | 32768 | 201.3 MB | 25.2 MB | 50.3 MB  | 0.094 |\n55\t| 4 | 2c1d8396 | 2  | 1024 | 2048 | 16384 | 100.7 MB | 12.6 MB | 25.2 MB  | 0.093 |\n56\t| 5 | 8b677344 | 4  | 512  | 2048 | 16384 | 100.7 MB | 12.6 MB | 25.2 MB  | 0.093 |\n57\t\n58\t`rtol = 0.05` for all. Workload 2 dominates cost; workload 1 is launch-overhead bound.\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## 2. Roofline / cost model\n63\t\n64\tZero real FLOPs (one add per element). This is **purely memory-bandwidth + atomic-contention\n65\tbound**. The unavoidable HBM floor per invocation:\n66\t\n67\t- read `expert_outputs`: `N*H*2 = 8*M*H*2` bytes (dominant — 8× the base),\n68\t- read base once + write output once: `~2 * M*H*2` bytes.\n69\t\n70\tSo `src` read is ~8/10 of the essential traffic and is identical for **any** correct\n71\timplementation (torch or ours). The reference also streams `src` exactly once. Therefore the\n72\tonly place we can win is:\n73\t\n74\t1. the **atomic accumulation cost** (torch `index_add_` on bf16 uses a bit-level CAS loop for\n75\t   `gpuAtomicAdd(bfloat16)` on `sm_80` — bf16 has no native global atomic add before Hopper),\n76\t   and contention amplifies that; and\n77\t2. **L2 residency** of the accumulator (`out`/scratch is 3–100 MB vs 40 MB L2), which decides\n78\t   whether the RMW traffic stays on-chip or spills to HBM.\n79\t\n80\tEstimated lower bound for the dominant W2: `~453 MB / 2 TB/s ≈ 0.23 ms` if atomics are cheap.\n81\tThe opportunity vs torch: replace slow bf16-CAS atomics with **native fp32 global atomics**\n82\t(single-instruction `red.global.add.f32` on Ampere) and better block/vectorization.\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 3. Numerical analysis & risk\n87\t\n88\t### 3.1 How the reference accumulates\n89\t`index_add_` on CUDA uses non-deterministic **atomic** ordering. For bf16 it is a CAS loop that\n90\treads bf16 → widens → adds → **rounds back to bf16 after every single add**. So the reference is:\n91\t\n92\t```\n93\tacc = round_bf16( ... round_bf16(round_bf16(base + s_{p0}) + s_{p1}) ... )\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\tfor a *random permutation* `p` of the ~8 source rows hitting a given output row. The reference\n97\tis therefore itself only an approximation of the true sum, it is **run-to-run non-deterministic\n98\tin the low bits**, and its error grows with the number of collisions and the magnitude.\n99\t\n100\t### 3.2 Our choice: accumulate in fp32, round once\n101\tIf we accumulate all contributions in **fp32** and round to bf16 exactly once at the end, our\n102\tresult is essentially the *true* sum rounded to bf16 — strictly **more accurate** than the\n103\treference. The deviation `|ours − ref|` is bounded by the reference's own bf16 accumulation\n104\terror, i.e. we inherit the reference's error budget, not add to it.\n105\t\n106\tMagnitude sanity check: base ~ N(0,1); each output row gets ~8 additions of N(0,1) values, so\n107\ttypical row magnitude ~ `sqrt(1 + 8) ≈ 3`, occasional |value| up to ~6–8. bf16 ulp at |x|≈4 is\n108\t`2^(2-8) = 0.0156`; ~8 sequential roundings → worst-case reference error ~0.05–0.12 in absolute\n109\tterms, but where that occurs `|ref|` is large so `rtol*|ref| = 0.05*4 = 0.2` dominates. Under the\n110\tstandard `allclose` rule `|a-b| <= atol + rtol*|b|`, the given `atol≈0.09..0.10` + `rtol=0.05`\n111\tcomfortably covers the difference between a once-rounded fp32 sum and a repeatedly-rounded bf16\n112\tsum. **Risk: LOW**, and fp32 accumulation is the safe direction (we err toward more accuracy).\n113\t\n114\t### 3.3 Tolerance-semantics caveat\n115\tI will assume the correctness check is `torch.allclose(actual, ref, atol=max_atol, rtol=max_rtol)`\n116\t= `|actual-ref| <= atol + rtol*|ref|`. If instead the harness checks *max absolute* AND *max\n117\trelative* error **separately** (stricter, no additive coupling), the near-zero rows are the risk:\n118\twhere `|ref|≈0`, `rtol*|ref|≈0`, so only `atol` protects us. Our fp32 result at those rows is\n119\tstill within ~a few bf16 ulps of the reference (both are small sums), well under `atol≈0.09`.\n120\tEither interpretation looks safe; I will treat the near-zero, high-collision rows as the\n121\tcorrectness stress case during validation reasoning.\n122\t\n123\t### 3.4 Correctness edge cases to guard\n124\t- **Rows with zero contributions** must equal `base` exactly (init must copy base for *all* M\n125\t  rows, not only touched ones). With 8× oversampling most rows are hit, but some are not.\n126\t- **Fresh output tensor**: reference `clone`s; we must **not** mutate `final_hidden_states`\n127\t  in place (the harness may reuse inputs across timing repeats). Allocate a new output.\n128\t- **int64 indices**: destination offset `idx*H` can reach `8191*3072 ≈ 25.2M` — fits int32 for\n129\t  these workloads, but I will use int64 offset arithmetic in pointers to be safe/general.\n130\t- **No out-of-range indices** (generator uses `randint(0, M)`), so no masking of indices needed;\n131\t  only the hidden-dim tail needs masking if `H` is not a multiple of the block.\n132\t- `H = 3072 = 3*1024` — clean multiples exist (512, 768, 1024, 1536, 3072), so hidden-tail\n133\t  masking may be avoidable with the right block, but I will keep a mask for safety.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 4. Triton design space\n138\t\n139\tCommon floor: `expert_outputs` must be streamed once (~8/10 of traffic) in every variant. The\n140\tvariants differ only in the accumulator representation, atomic type, and number of passes.\n141\t\n142\t### Option A — Direct bf16 atomic scatter (mimics reference)\n143\tOne program per `(source row, hidden block)`: load `idx`, load `src` block, `tl.atomic_add`\n144\tinto a bf16 `output` pre-filled with `base`.\n145\t- Pros: minimal passes (clone base + scatter); output is only 3–50 MB → best L2 residency;\n146\t  numerically identical style to reference.\n147\t- Cons: bf16 global atomics on `sm_80` compile to a **CAS loop** (Triton emits a `atom.cas`\n148\t  retry loop for bf16) — slow, and contention (avg 8 hits/row) amplifies retries. Likely no\n149\t  better than torch, which does the same thing.\n150\t- Verdict: baseline/reference-parity candidate; probably **not** the winner but cheap to test.\n151\t\n152\t### Option B — fp32-scratch native atomic scatter  ← primary candidate\n153\t1. Allocate an fp32 scratch `buf[M, H]`; **init** `buf = base` (Triton cast-copy kernel).\n154\t2. **Scatter**: one program per `(source row, hidden block)` → `tl.atomic_add` (native\n155\t   `red.global.add.f32`) `src.to(fp32)` into `buf[idx]`.\n156\t3. **Finalize**: Triton kernel casts `buf` → bf16 `output` (fresh tensor).\n157\t- Pros: **native fp32 atomics** (single instruction, fast even under contention); fp32\n158\t  accumulation → most accurate → safest vs tolerance; entirely Triton-native (rule-clean).\n159\t- Cons: extra passes (init read 50 + write 100; finalize read 100 + write 50 for W2 ≈ +300 MB)\n160\t  and the fp32 scratch (50–100 MB) exceeds L2 for W2/W3 → atomic RMW spills to HBM.\n161\t- Micro-variants to try:\n162\t  - **B0**: init `buf=base`, scatter, cast. (base read once in init.)\n163\t  - **B1**: init `buf=0` (memset, write-only), scatter, finalize `out = round(base + buf)`\n164\t    (fused: read base + read buf, write out). Slightly cheaper init.\n165\t- Verdict: **strongest, safest first candidate (c001).** Expected win comes from native fp32\n166\t  atomics replacing torch's bf16 CAS loop.\n167\t\n168\t### Option C — fp32 atomics, fused init via \"seed\" trick\n169\tAvoid a separate init pass by seeding: run scatter into a zeroed buf, then in finalize add base.\n170\tThis is exactly B1. Keeps only 2 kernels beyond memset. Worth measuring against B0.\n171\t\n172\t### Option D — Sort / argsort → segmented gather (no atomics)\n173\tPrecompute `perm = argsort(token_indices)` and per-row offsets, then a Triton kernel gathers each\n174\trow's contiguous run of contributions and reduces in fp32 (no atomics, deterministic).\n175\t- Pros: no atomic contention; perfectly deterministic; single accurate reduction.\n176\t- Cons: **`argsort`/`sort`/`bincount`/`cumsum` are non-trivial Torch computation** — likely a\n177\t  rule violation (\"PyTorch only for metadata and launch plumbing\"; \"no alternate implementation\n178\t  fallback\"). Also adds a sort (~N log N) whose overhead can dominate small workloads.\n179\t- Verdict: **avoid** for rule-compliance reasons; keep only as a last-resort idea and only if a\n180\t  Triton-native sort/segmentation is feasible (it is not, cheaply). **Not pursued.**\n181\t\n182\t### Option E — Load-balanced / larger-tile scatter (tuning layer on B)\n183\tOn top of Option B: tune the hidden block (`H=3072` → BLOCK ∈ {512,768,1024,1536,3072}),\n184\t`num_warps`, `num_stages`; consider **one program per full source row** (BLOCK=3072, single\n185\t`idx` load per row, 6 KB streamed load) vs multiple hidden blocks per row (more parallelism,\n186\tmore redundant `idx` loads). Also consider vectorized loads and `.to(tl.float32)` on load.\n187\t- Verdict: the real performance search happens here once B is correct.\n188\t\n189\t### Chosen ordering for candidates (to be finalized in plan.md)\n190\t1. **c001 = Option B0** (fp32 scratch, native atomics, 3 Triton kernels) — correctness + baseline win.\n191\t2. **c002 = Option B1** (memset init + fused base-add finalize) — fewer bytes.\n192\t3. **c003+** = block/warps/stages autotune, full-row vs tiled, dtype-on-load tweaks (Option E).\n193\t4. Optionally **Option A** (bf16 direct atomics) as a comparison point for L2-residency effect on\n194\t   small workloads (W1) where the accumulator fits in cache.\n195\t\n196\tNote: `tcgen05`/TMA/warp-specialization/CLC etc. are Hopper/Blackwell features — **not applicable\n197\tto `sm_80`**; the KernelWiki skill is out of scope for this task and will not be used.\n198\t\n199\t---\n200\t\n201\t## 5. Key implementation details / pitfalls\n202\t\n203\t- **Grid**: scatter grid `(N, cdiv(H, BLOCK))` (or `(N,)` for full-row). N up to 65536, so up to\n204\t  ~393k programs — fine.\n205\t- **Index load**: load `token_indices[pid_n]` as int64 scalar; compute row base offset\n206\t  `idx * H` in int64; add hidden offsets; mask hidden tail (`offs_h < H`).\n207\t- **Atomic**: `tl.atomic_add(buf_ptr + row*H + offs_h, val_f32, mask=...)`. Confirm Triton\n208\t  version emits native `red/atom.global.add.f32` on `sm_80` (expected). For Option A confirm\n209\t  whether bf16 `atomic_add` is even supported / how it lowers.\n210\t- **No in-place mutation** of inputs; allocate `output` and `buf` fresh via `torch.empty`.\n211\t- **Init correctness**: every one of the M rows must be initialized (cover full `[M,H]` in the\n212\t  init/finalize grids, mask tails).\n213\t- **Determinism**: our fp32 path is deterministic; reference is not — expect tiny, tolerance-safe\n214\t  disagreement, and do not chase bit-exactness.\n215\t- **Contention hotspots**: random duplicates → some rows hit >8×; fp32 native atomics serialize\n216\t  cheaply. Acceptable. Avoid designs whose per-element retry cost blows up (bf16 CAS).\n217\t- **Small-workload (W1) regime**: everything L2-resident; kernel-launch overhead dominates.\n218\t  Minimize number of kernel launches (favor B1's 3 launches; watch that extra passes don't\n219\t  regress W1). This is a per-workload tension to watch in evidence.\n220\t\n221\t---\n222\t\n223\t## 6. Validation strategy\n224\t\n225\t1. **Correctness (per candidate, all 5 workloads)** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`\n226\t   only — never a private harness, never direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi. The evaluator applies\n227\t   the per-workload `max_atol`/`max_rtol`.\n228\t2. **Numerical pre-reasoning** before each eval: confirm (a) all M rows initialized to base,\n229\t   (b) fresh output, (c) fp32 accumulation → deviation ≤ reference's own bf16 error ≤ tolerance,\n230\t   (d) hidden-tail masking correct. Stress-focus on near-zero and high-collision rows (§3.3).\n231\t3. **Performance**: geomean speedup across the 5 workloads is the ranking metric; every workload\n232\t   must pass correctness. Record per-workload speedup, watch the small-workload (W1) launch-overhead\n233\t   regime and the large-workload (W2) L2-spill regime separately, since they favor different\n234\t   variants (bf16-cached vs fp32-native-atomic).\n235\t4. **A/B discipline**: change exactly one meaningful thing per candidate ID; never reuse an ID for\n236\t   changed source; append one JSON record per eval to `candidates.jsonl` (parent, source hash,\n237\t   hypothesis, per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage).\n238\t5. **Convergence / stop**: stop at budget (100 evals / 1.0M token soft / 1.2M hard) or when the\n239\t   geomean plateaus; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. `final` only with explicit operator approval.\n240\t\n241\t### Open questions to resolve empirically (via candidate evals)\n242\t- Does native fp32-scratch (B) actually beat torch given +300 MB of extra passes? (Expected yes,\n243\t  from atomic-cost savings — confirm on W2.)\n244\t- B0 vs B1 (init-copy vs memset+fused-add): which wins net bytes/launches?\n245\t- Does bf16-direct atomics (A) win on small/L2-resident W1 despite CAS loops?\n246\t- Best hidden BLOCK / full-row vs tiled / num_warps / num_stages for the scatter kernel.\n247\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f843108-68c0-43be-addb-b7c1183c67b7","uuid":"7dbf01d6-58fb-47e4-a485-e8d3e0d9d65f","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:31:32.602Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-008_expert_output_weighted_index_add_accumulation/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/008 Expert Output Weighted Index-Add Accumulation\n\nTask ID: `sol_execbench :: L1/008_expert_output_weighted_index_add_accumulation`\nTarget GPU: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere), HBM2e ~2.0 TB/s, L2 = 40 MB.\nPrimary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata / launch plumbing.\nNo Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation actually computes\n\nReference (`task/definition.json`):\n\n```python\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef run(final_hidden_states, expert_outputs, token_indices):\n    output = final_hidden_states.clone()\n    output.index_add_(dim=0, index=token_indices, source=expert_outputs)\n    return output\n```\n\nSemantically, for `i in [0, num_selected_tokens)`:\n\n```\noutput[token_indices[i], :] += expert_outputs[i, :]\n```\n\nwith the base value `final_hidden_states` copied first, and **atomic add** semantics\nbecause the destination indices contain duplicates (top-k routing → many source rows\nmap to the same token row).\n\n### Tensors / dtypes / shapes\n\n| name                 | shape                          | dtype    | role                          |\n|----------------------|--------------------------------|----------|-------------------------------|\n| `final_hidden_states`| `[M, H]`                       | bf16     | base accumulation buffer      |\n| `expert_outputs`     | `[N, H]`                       | bf16     | per-selected-token weighted output (already weighted) |\n| `token_indices`      | `[N]`                          | int64    | destination row for each source row |\n| `output` (return)    | `[M, H]`                       | bf16     | `clone(base)` + scatter-add   |\n\nConstants: `H = hidden_size = 3072`, `num_experts_per_tok = 8`.\nRelations: `M = batch_seq_len = batch_size * seq_len`, `N = num_selected_tokens = 8 * M`.\n\nImportant: the routing weight multiply has **already been folded into** `expert_outputs`\n(the description says \"weighted outputs\"; `run` does a plain `index_add_`). So this kernel\nis a pure **scatter-add**, not a fused multiply-scatter. No extra weight tensor is provided.\n\n### Feedback workload sizes\n\n| # | uuid | batch | seq | M (rows) | N=8M | src read (bf16) | out (bf16) | fp32 scratch | atol |\n|---|------|-------|-----|----------|------|-----------------|------------|--------------|------|\n| 1 | ada14c03 | 2  | 256  | 512  | 4096  | 25.2 MB  | 3.1 MB  | 6.3 MB   | 0.087 |\n| 2 | 8c5c94b5 | 32 | 256  | 8192 | 65536 | 402.7 MB | 50.3 MB | 100.7 MB | 0.100 |\n| 3 | b61ee9a7 | 16 | 256  | 4096 | 32768 | 201.3 MB | 25.2 MB | 50.3 MB  | 0.094 |\n| 4 | 2c1d8396 | 2  | 1024 | 2048 | 16384 | 100.7 MB | 12.6 MB | 25.2 MB  | 0.093 |\n| 5 | 8b677344 | 4  | 512  | 2048 | 16384 | 100.7 MB | 12.6 MB | 25.2 MB  | 0.093 |\n\n`rtol = 0.05` for all. Workload 2 dominates cost; workload 1 is launch-overhead bound.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Roofline / cost model\n\nZero real FLOPs (one add per element). This is **purely memory-bandwidth + atomic-contention\nbound**. The unavoidable HBM floor per invocation:\n\n- read `expert_outputs`: `N*H*2 = 8*M*H*2` bytes (dominant — 8× the base),\n- read base once + write output once: `~2 * M*H*2` bytes.\n\nSo `src` read is ~8/10 of the essential traffic and is identical for **any** correct\nimplementation (torch or ours). The reference also streams `src` exactly once. Therefore the\nonly place we can win is:\n\n1. the **atomic accumulation cost** (torch `index_add_` on bf16 uses a bit-level CAS loop for\n   `gpuAtomicAdd(bfloat16)` on `sm_80` — bf16 has no native global atomic add before Hopper),\n   and contention amplifies that; and\n2. **L2 residency** of the accumulator (`out`/scratch is 3–100 MB vs 40 MB L2), which decides\n   whether the RMW traffic stays on-chip or spills to HBM.\n\nEstimated lower bound for the dominant W2: `~453 MB / 2 TB/s ≈ 0.23 ms` if atomics are cheap.\nThe opportunity vs torch: replace slow bf16-CAS atomics with **native fp32 global atomics**\n(single-instruction `red.global.add.f32` on Ampere) and better block/vectorization.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Numerical analysis & risk\n\n### 3.1 How the reference accumulates\n`index_add_` on CUDA uses non-deterministic **atomic** ordering. For bf16 it is a CAS loop that\nreads bf16 → widens → adds → **rounds back to bf16 after every single add**. So the reference is:\n\n```\nacc = round_bf16( ... round_bf16(round_bf16(base + s_{p0}) + s_{p1}) ... )\n```\n\nfor a *random permutation* `p` of the ~8 source rows hitting a given output row. The reference\nis therefore itself only an approximation of the true sum, it is **run-to-run non-deterministic\nin the low bits**, and its error grows with the number of collisions and the magnitude.\n\n### 3.2 Our choice: accumulate in fp32, round once\nIf we accumulate all contributions in **fp32** and round to bf16 exactly once at the end, our\nresult is essentially the *true* sum rounded to bf16 — strictly **more accurate** than the\nreference. The deviation `|ours − ref|` is bounded by the reference's own bf16 accumulation\nerror, i.e. we inherit the reference's error budget, not add to it.\n\nMagnitude sanity check: base ~ N(0,1); each output row gets ~8 additions of N(0,1) values, so\ntypical row magnitude ~ `sqrt(1 + 8) ≈ 3`, occasional |value| up to ~6–8. bf16 ulp at |x|≈4 is\n`2^(2-8) = 0.0156`; ~8 sequential roundings → worst-case reference error ~0.05–0.12 in absolute\nterms, but where that occurs `|ref|` is large so `rtol*|ref| = 0.05*4 = 0.2` dominates. Under the\nstandard `allclose` rule `|a-b| <= atol + rtol*|b|`, the given `atol≈0.09..0.10` + `rtol=0.05`\ncomfortably covers the difference between a once-rounded fp32 sum and a repeatedly-rounded bf16\nsum. **Risk: LOW**, and fp32 accumulation is the safe direction (we err toward more accuracy).\n\n### 3.3 Tolerance-semantics caveat\nI will assume the correctness check is `torch.allclose(actual, ref, atol=max_atol, rtol=max_rtol)`\n= `|actual-ref| <= atol + rtol*|ref|`. If instead the harness checks *max absolute* AND *max\nrelative* error **separately** (stricter, no additive coupling), the near-zero rows are the risk:\nwhere `|ref|≈0`, `rtol*|ref|≈0`, so only `atol` protects us. Our fp32 result at those rows is\nstill within ~a few bf16 ulps of the reference (both are small sums), well under `atol≈0.09`.\nEither interpretation looks safe; I will treat the near-zero, high-collision rows as the\ncorrectness stress case during validation reasoning.\n\n### 3.4 Correctness edge cases to guard\n- **Rows with zero contributions** must equal `base` exactly (init must copy base for *all* M\n  rows, not only touched ones). With 8× oversampling most rows are hit, but some are not.\n- **Fresh output tensor**: reference `clone`s; we must **not** mutate `final_hidden_states`\n  in place (the harness may reuse inputs across timing repeats). Allocate a new output.\n- **int64 indices**: destination offset `idx*H` can reach `8191*3072 ≈ 25.2M` — fits int32 for\n  these workloads, but I will use int64 offset arithmetic in pointers to be safe/general.\n- **No out-of-range indices** (generator uses `randint(0, M)`), so no masking of indices needed;\n  only the hidden-dim tail needs masking if `H` is not a multiple of the block.\n- `H = 3072 = 3*1024` — clean multiples exist (512, 768, 1024, 1536, 3072), so hidden-tail\n  masking may be avoidable with the right block, but I will keep a mask for safety.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\nCommon floor: `expert_outputs` must be streamed once (~8/10 of traffic) in every variant. The\nvariants differ only in the accumulator representation, atomic type, and number of passes.\n\n### Option A — Direct bf16 atomic scatter (mimics reference)\nOne program per `(source row, hidden block)`: load `idx`, load `src` block, `tl.atomic_add`\ninto a bf16 `output` pre-filled with `base`.\n- Pros: minimal passes (clone base + scatter); output is only 3–50 MB → best L2 residency;\n  numerically identical style to reference.\n- Cons: bf16 global atomics on `sm_80` compile to a **CAS loop** (Triton emits a `atom.cas`\n  retry loop for bf16) — slow, and contention (avg 8 hits/row) amplifies retries. Likely no\n  better than torch, which does the same thing.\n- Verdict: baseline/reference-parity candidate; probably **not** the winner but cheap to test.\n\n### Option B — fp32-scratch native atomic scatter  ← primary candidate\n1. Allocate an fp32 scratch `buf[M, H]`; **init** `buf = base` (Triton cast-copy kernel).\n2. **Scatter**: one program per `(source row, hidden block)` → `tl.atomic_add` (native\n   `red.global.add.f32`) `src.to(fp32)` into `buf[idx]`.\n3. **Finalize**: Triton kernel casts `buf` → bf16 `output` (fresh tensor).\n- Pros: **native fp32 atomics** (single instruction, fast even under contention); fp32\n  accumulation → most accurate → safest vs tolerance; entirely Triton-native (rule-clean).\n- Cons: extra passes (init read 50 + write 100; finalize read 100 + write 50 for W2 ≈ +300 MB)\n  and the fp32 scratch (50–100 MB) exceeds L2 for W2/W3 → atomic RMW spills to HBM.\n- Micro-variants to try:\n  - **B0**: init `buf=base`, scatter, cast. (base read once in init.)\n  - **B1**: init `buf=0` (memset, write-only), scatter, finalize `out = round(base + buf)`\n    (fused: read base + read buf, write out). Slightly cheaper init.\n- Verdict: **strongest, safest first candidate (c001).** Expected win comes from native fp32\n  atomics replacing torch's bf16 CAS loop.\n\n### Option C — fp32 atomics, fused init via \"seed\" trick\nAvoid a separate init pass by seeding: run scatter into a zeroed buf, then in finalize add base.\nThis is exactly B1. Keeps only 2 kernels beyond memset. Worth measuring against B0.\n\n### Option D — Sort / argsort → segmented gather (no atomics)\nPrecompute `perm = argsort(token_indices)` and per-row offsets, then a Triton kernel gathers each\nrow's contiguous run of contributions and reduces in fp32 (no atomics, deterministic).\n- Pros: no atomic contention; perfectly deterministic; single accurate reduction.\n- Cons: **`argsort`/`sort`/`bincount`/`cumsum` are non-trivial Torch computation** — likely a\n  rule violation (\"PyTorch only for metadata and launch plumbing\"; \"no alternate implementation\n  fallback\"). Also adds a sort (~N log N) whose overhead can dominate small workloads.\n- Verdict: **avoid** for rule-compliance reasons; keep only as a last-resort idea and only if a\n  Triton-native sort/segmentation is feasible (it is not, cheaply). **Not pursued.**\n\n### Option E — Load-balanced / larger-tile scatter (tuning layer on B)\nOn top of Option B: tune the hidden block (`H=3072` → BLOCK ∈ {512,768,1024,1536,3072}),\n`num_warps`, `num_stages`; consider **one program per full source row** (BLOCK=3072, single\n`idx` load per row, 6 KB streamed load) vs multiple hidden blocks per row (more parallelism,\nmore redundant `idx` loads). Also consider vectorized loads and `.to(tl.float32)` on load.\n- Verdict: the real performance search happens here once B is correct.\n\n### Chosen ordering for candidates (to be finalized in plan.md)\n1. **c001 = Option B0** (fp32 scratch, native atomics, 3 Triton kernels) — correctness + baseline win.\n2. **c002 = Option B1** (memset init + fused base-add finalize) — fewer bytes.\n3. **c003+** = block/warps/stages autotune, full-row vs tiled, dtype-on-load tweaks (Option E).\n4. Optionally **Option A** (bf16 direct atomics) as a comparison point for L2-residency effect on\n   small workloads (W1) where the accumulator fits in cache.\n\nNote: `tcgen05`/TMA/warp-specialization/CLC etc. are Hopper/Blackwell features — **not applicable\nto `sm_80`**; the KernelWiki skill is out of scope for this task and will not be used.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Key implementation details / pitfalls\n\n- **Grid**: scatter grid `(N, cdiv(H, BLOCK))` (or `(N,)` for full-row). N up to 65536, so up to\n  ~393k programs — fine.\n- **Index load**: load `token_indices[pid_n]` as int64 scalar; compute row base offset\n  `idx * H` in int64; add hidden offsets; mask hidden tail (`offs_h < H`).\n- **Atomic**: `tl.atomic_add(buf_ptr + row*H + offs_h, val_f32, mask=...)`. Confirm Triton\n  version emits native `red/atom.global.add.f32` on `sm_80` (expected). For Option A confirm\n  whether bf16 `atomic_add` is even supported / how it lowers.\n- **No in-place mutation** of inputs; allocate `output` and `buf` fresh via `torch.empty`.\n- **Init correctness**: every one of the M rows must be initialized (cover full `[M,H]` in the\n  init/finalize grids, mask tails).\n- **Determinism**: our fp32 path is deterministic; reference is not — expect tiny, tolerance-safe\n  disagreement, and do not chase bit-exactness.\n- **Contention hotspots**: random duplicates → some rows hit >8×; fp32 native atomics serialize\n  cheaply. Acceptable. Avoid designs whose per-element retry cost blows up (bf16 CAS).\n- **Small-workload (W1) regime**: everything L2-resident; kernel-launch overhead dominates.\n  Minimize number of kernel launches (favor B1's 3 launches; watch that extra passes don't\n  regress W1). This is a per-workload tension to watch in evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation strategy\n\n1. **Correctness (per candidate, all 5 workloads)** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`\n   only — never a private harness, never direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi. The evaluator applies\n   the per-workload `max_atol`/`max_rtol`.\n2. **Numerical pre-reasoning** before each eval: confirm (a) all M rows initialized to base,\n   (b) fresh output, (c) fp32 accumulation → deviation ≤ reference's own bf16 error ≤ tolerance,\n   (d) hidden-tail masking correct. Stress-focus on near-zero and high-collision rows (§3.3).\n3. **Performance**: geomean speedup across the 5 workloads is the ranking metric; every workload\n   must pass correctness. Record per-workload speedup, watch the small-workload (W1) launch-overhead\n   regime and the large-workload (W2) L2-spill regime separately, since they favor different\n   variants (bf16-cached vs fp32-native-atomic).\n4. **A/B discipline**: change exactly one meaningful thing per candidate ID; never reuse an ID for\n   changed source; append one JSON record per eval to `candidates.jsonl` (parent, source hash,\n   hypothesis, per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage).\n5. **Convergence / stop**: stop at budget (100 evals / 1.0M token soft / 1.2M hard) or when the\n   geomean plateaus; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. `final` only with explicit operator approval.\n\n### Open questions to resolve empirically (via candidate evals)\n- Does native fp32-scratch (B) actually beat torch given +300 MB of extra passes? (Expected yes,\n  from atomic-cost savings — confirm on W2.)\n- B0 vs B1 (init-copy vs memset+fused-add): which wins net bytes/launches?\n- Does bf16-direct atomics (A) win on small/L2-resident W1 despite CAS loops?\n- Best hidden BLOCK / full-row vs tiled / num_warps / num_stages for the scatter kernel.\n","numLines":247,"startLine":1,"totalLines":247}}}
